WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:01.950
- Swearing gets a bad rap.

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:04.075
For a long time, it was associtated.

00:00:04.075 --> 00:00:05.680
(BEEP) Let me try again.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:07.350
Swearing gets a bad rap.

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.673
For a long time, it was associate.

00:00:09.673 --> 00:00:11.880
(BEEP) Swearing gets a bad rap.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.391
For a long time, it was associ.

00:00:14.391 --> 00:00:17.550
(BEEP) Mind is a
piece of (BEEP) today.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:20.210
For a long time, it was
associated only with poor

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:21.670
or low-class people.

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:24.960
In fact, the word vulgar
originally just meant common.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:26.500
But if you've ever watched "Succession,"

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.340
you know that today the rich and powerful

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:30.396
drop plenty of F-bombs.

00:00:30.396 --> 00:00:31.935
- I'm not dignifying that (BEEP)

00:00:31.935 --> 00:00:32.768
question with an answer.

00:00:32.768 --> 00:00:33.700
You can both (BEEP) off.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.840
- Despite their popularity,
the gatekeepers of language

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.330
often like to pretend that
bad words don't exist.

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:42.460
Dictionaries and etymologies ignored them

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:45.230
for hundreds of years, which is why today

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:47.380
it's so hard to tell how they originated.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.290
Anything that even
sounds like a swear word

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:51.980
gets scrubbed from acceptable vocabulary,

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:54.470
which is why donkey and rooster

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:56.300
have replaced their forerunners.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.757
Even Shakespeare had his cusses
removed from some editions.

00:00:59.757 --> 00:01:01.130
- Zounds, What a tragedy!

00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:03.970
- But so-called bad words
are a fundamental part

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:06.530
of human vocabulary and
there is some evidence

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:09.460
that using them in moderation may actually

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.030
be good for your psyche.

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:15.318
I'm Dr. Erica Brozovsky and
this is Outerwords. Otherwords.

00:01:15.318 --> 00:01:16.663
(BEEP) We'll do it live.

00:01:19.166 --> 00:01:21.749
(upbeat music)

00:01:26.347 --> 00:01:28.430
- [Announcer] Otherwords.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:32.270
- Even the cleanest mouths
among us have stepped on a Lego

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:35.260
or gotten some bad news
and let an expletive slip.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.960
Linguists call this
non-propositional swearing,

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:41.020
meaning unplanned, almost
involuntary outbursts.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:44.200
For a long time, psychologists
discouraged this type

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.500
of swearing because it
thought it catastrophized

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.660
negative emotions, meaning
it made you feel even worse.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:52.990
But then why would we all have
this reflexive urge to do it?

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:54.580
Ow, son of a (BEEP).

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.540
This is what psychologist Dr.
Richard Stephens wondered.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:00.770
And he sought to test it
with a very cool experiment.

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:03.000
Participants were asked to
hold their hands in buckets

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.500
of ice water for as long as they could,

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:06.960
once while repeating a swear word

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.770
and once with a neutral word.

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:10.620
No matter what order they went in,

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:12.730
swearing increased the
average amount of time

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:15.600
they could withstand the pain by 50%.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.200
The swearing was acting as some kind

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.160
of pain management mechanism.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.410
Subsequent studies bore
these findings out.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:23.210
And there was evidence
that swearing was effective

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:26.250
at lessening social and
emotional pain as well.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.280
In some experiments, it
seemed that the naughtier

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.650
the word was, the more effective it was.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.900
So saying shoot or gosh
darn was like taking

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:34.970
children's Tylenol, better than nothing

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:36.930
but not as good as the real deal.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:40.850
Like medicinal painkillers,
they can also be overused.

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:42.910
People who tended to swear a lot got less

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:43.920
of a benefit from it.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.320
To understand what the F is going on here,

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:50.330
we'll have to take a look
inside the human brain.

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:52.020
Most people know that the brain is divided

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.970
into two hemispheres, the
left side which specializes

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:57.570
in higher order reasoning
and the right side

00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:59.920
which is all about emotion and creativity.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.320
In reality, it's quite a bit
more complicated than that

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:03.960
but you get the idea.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.510
Most of our language functions reside

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:08.750
in the left hemisphere, which
makes sense because a language

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:11.240
involves the arrangement
of complex thoughts.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:12.990
Swearing however seems to rely more

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:14.610
on the right side of the brain.

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:17.460
This has been witnessed by
neuroscientists in real life.

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.500
People who suffer massive
damage to the left side

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:22.150
of the brain often lose
significant language ability,

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:23.900
a condition known as aphasia.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.330
However, many of them retain
their ability to swear

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:28.160
and tend to swear more often.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:30.630
Conversely, many who have
suffered damage to the right side

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:32.740
of the brain can still
speak clearly and coherently

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:36.410
but seem to lose the
ability or urge to swear.

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:39.060
Swearing, at least the
non-propositional kind,

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.220
is clearly more of an emotional reflex

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.040
than a rational behavior.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.250
That's why some linguists
think it's more akin

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:47.370
to a dog's bark or a lion's growl

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:48.790
than human language.

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:50.670
It quickens the heartbeat, dulls pain,

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.400
gets us ready for action.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.370
And because it's so
associated with emotion,

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:58.030
swearing can actually help build trust.

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:00.460
Studies show that groups
that swear conversationally

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:02.930
tend to forge stronger
bonds and perform better

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:04.560
at shared tasks.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:07.850
Displays of emotion like swearing
are generally interpreted

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:11.590
to be more genuine and harder
to fake than ordinary speech.

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:14.700
And breaking taboos in front
of other people implies a level

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:16.820
of shared trust and understanding.

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:19.030
Swearing can even minimize
the potential for violence

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:21.060
by offering an outlet for aggression.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.620
Instead of throwing
punches or pulling hair,

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:26.200
we can show how (BEEP) we
are with words instead.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.720
Such displays of aggression
as a substitute for violence

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:30.900
are pretty common
throughout the animal world.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.010
The words we consider bad give us power

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:34.730
because they are taboo.

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:38.100
But if you go back 500 years,
it was the other way around.

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:39.320
Bad words were considered taboo

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:41.580
because people believed they had power.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:44.600
Back then naughty language was
focused on God and religion.

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:47.100
Invoking the deity's name,
swearing by something sacred,

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.390
or cursing someone were
supposed to have real effects

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:50.990
in the real world.

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:52.860
This is why we call them swears, oaths,

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:54.590
and curses or cusses.

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:57.483
Some of Shakespeare's favorites,
'sblood, 'slid, zounds,

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:01.170
were shortening of God's
blood, God's eyelid,

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:02.770
and God's wounds.

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:04.900
If you're swearing by God's body parts,

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:06.810
the next thing you say better be true

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:08.730
or your soul may pay for it.

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:11.230
And you didn't say "damn you" or "go to hell"

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:13.050
unless you really wanted someone

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:14.943
to go to hell, like literally.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.950
As culture became more secular,
taboos shifted from religion

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:24.420
to things like sex,
excrement, and body parts,

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:26.110
though it's hardly universal.

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:29.220
In Germany, some of the
worst swears involve animals,

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:31.600
calling people dogs or pigs.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:34.580
And in Japan, there's not
such a stigma around feces.

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:37.270
In fact, they seem to
find it kind of cute.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:39.840
So having a (BEEP)
might be a good thing.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:41.840
Whatever the taboo is, the important thing

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:43.150
is that it's shared.

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:45.560
We've all collectively
agreed to put certain words

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:49.000
behind glass, not so
that they're never used

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:51.530
but so that we can know when
(BEEP) gets real.

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:54.100
It may not be the most
sophisticated form of expression

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.290
but there's a primal magic to swearing

00:05:56.290 --> 00:05:58.830
that's still with us
after thousands of years.

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:01.420
And like any magic, if
you use it too much,

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:03.020
it can lose its powder.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:06.350
Power! (BEEP)

00:06:06.350 --> 00:06:07.810
Wait, before you go.

00:06:07.810 --> 00:06:10.070
